<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Cisalpine Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1797 CE – 1802 CE · capital Milan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Napoleon's largest Italian sister republic, uniting Lombardy, Emilia and Romagna, and an early laboratory for a modern Italian state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 29 June 1797 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> proclaimed the Cisalpine Republic, merging the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/transpadane-republic">Transpadane Republic</a> with territories that soon included the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cispadane-republic">Cispadane Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valtellina">Valtellina</a> and the former Venetian cities of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-bergamo">Bergamo</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-brescia">Brescia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crema">Crema</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> confirmed it in October 1797.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic had a constitution modelled on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-first-republic">French Directory</a>, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Milan</a> as capital. It reorganised its territory into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, sold church lands and built a national guard, but the French made and unmade its governments and drew heavy contributions. Debate about Italian identity flourished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>An Austro-Russian offensive overthrew the republic in 1799. Restored after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marengo">Marengo</a> in 1800, it was transformed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consulta-of-lyon">Consulta of Lyon</a> in January 1802 into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-republic-1802-1805">Italian Republic</a> (1802–1805).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cisalpine-republic.jpg" alt="Cisalpine Republic" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: PavelD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Repubblica_Cisalpina.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1797 CE – 1802 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Milan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Lombard, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>, <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Repubblica Cisalpina</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=cisalpine-republic" aria-label="Cisalpine Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4898 574 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cisalpine Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5218 830L5218 830M5218 830L5271 860M5218 830L5218 830M5218 830L5229 823M5218 830L5242 828M5218 830L5230 833M5218 830L5054 728M5218 830L5381 747M5218 830L5303 923"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5218 830h0M5271 860h0M5218 830h0M5229 823h0M5242 828h0M5230 833h0M5054 728h0M5381 747h0M5303 923h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Milan. <a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=cisalpine-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
